<%!-- The standalone canvas page (task 019e2c4b, stages 3 and 4).

     The two denial states differ in SHAPE, not only in copy: blur the text
     out and they are still telling you different things. 403 is a tinted card
     with one action; 404 is bare text with none.

     `--color-error` appears in NEITHER. Being denied a canvas is not a system
     failure, and red would teach viewers that a normal permission boundary is
     a bug. Error red stays reserved for `render_invalid` in
     `canvas_renderer.ex`, for a document that fails `canonical?/1`. --%>
<div class="flex h-full flex-col bg-base-100">
  <%= case @load_error do %>
    <% :forbidden -> %>
      <%!-- 403 — "this exists, you cannot see it yet." A door, not a wall,
           so it gets a surface of its own (`--radius-box` card, `--color-info`
           border and tint) and exactly ONE action.

           The action names the owner from the ADR-0038 creator snapshot. The
           place is named only to a viewer who is a member of that space's org;
           everyone else gets "a space in Fabrikam", so this page cannot be walked
           as a directory. See `assign_denial_context/4`. --%>
      <div
        class="flex flex-1 items-center justify-center px-6 py-12"
        data-role="canvas-load-error"
        data-error="forbidden"
      >
        <section
          class="w-full max-w-md rounded-box border border-info/30 bg-info/10 px-8 py-10 text-center"
          data-role="canvas-forbidden-card"
        >
          <span class="hero-lock-closed mx-auto block size-8 text-info"></span>

          <h1 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-base-content">
            You do not have access to this canvas
          </h1>

          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-neutral" data-role="canvas-forbidden-place">
            It exists in {@denial_place}.
          </p>

          <a
            href={~p"/chat"}
            class="btn btn-info btn-sm mt-6"
            data-role="canvas-denial-action"
          >
            Ask {@denial_owner} for access
          </a>

          <%!-- The quiet way out, matching the one the 404 already carries. A
               ghost link is not a primary action, so "exactly one primary
               action" still holds and the two states stay structurally
               distinct. Without it this was the only screen on the route with
               no exit at all. --%>
          <a
            href={~p"/chat"}
            class="mt-6 block text-xs text-neutral underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-base-content"
            data-role="canvas-forbidden-link"
          >
            Back to Suite
          </a>
        </section>
      </div>
    <% :not_found -> %>
      <%!-- 404 — a clean dead end should look like one. No card, no
           illustration, no tint: centred `--color-neutral` text on bare
           `--color-base-100`, one ghost link back to Suite and ZERO actions.
           Offering an action here would imply a path that does not exist. --%>
      <div
        class="flex flex-1 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-2 px-6 text-center"
        data-role="canvas-load-error"
        data-error="not_found"
      >
        <p class="text-sm text-neutral">This canvas does not exist.</p>

        <p class="text-xs text-neutral/70">
          The link may be wrong, or the canvas may have been deleted.
        </p>

        <a
          href={~p"/chat"}
          class="mt-4 text-xs text-neutral underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-base-content"
          data-role="canvas-not-found-link"
        >
          Back to Suite
        </a>
      </div>
    <% nil -> %>
      <%!-- The shell layout's <main> is `overflow-hidden`, so this surface
           supplies its own scroller. The provenance bar is `sticky top-0`
           INSIDE that scroller, which is what lets the document scroll under
           it. --%>
      <div class="min-h-0 flex-1 overflow-y-auto" data-role="canvas-standalone-scroller">
        <%!-- PROVENANCE BAR — the signature element and the only band of tint
           on the page, so it reads as chrome and everything below it reads as
           document. One `--color-base-300` hairline; no other dividers.

           Truncation order is the structural rule: the title is `shrink-0
           whitespace-nowrap` and truncates LAST, the crumb is `min-w-0
           truncate` and collapses FIRST, the pill is `shrink-0` and never
           wins space from the title.

           Mobile keeps one bar and reflows rather than growing an overflow
           menu: `basis-full` drops the crumb to its own line under the title
           while the pill, the copy control and the close control stay on line
           one. Hiding a one-tap copy or a one-tap exit behind a "…" costs a
           tap and buys nothing, and persistent controls beat reveal-on-tap.
           `order-*` restores the prescribed left-to-right reading order at
           `sm` and up.

           The title is `shrink-0` from `sm` up only. At phone width the crumb
           has ALREADY yielded its entire line, so the priority still holds —
           the title gives way last, after everything else has — and pinning it
           unshrinkable there would push the pill onto a line of its own
           instead of letting a long title ellipsise. Below `sm` it is
           `flex-1`, i.e. a ZERO flex-basis: on a wrapping row an item wider
           than the line is pushed onto its own line rather than shrunk, so
           `min-w-0 truncate` alone is not enough to keep the title, pill and
           copy control together. Zero-basis is what makes it truncate in
           place. --%>
        <header
          class="sticky top-0 z-10 border-b border-base-300 bg-base-200 px-4 py-2 sm:px-6"
          data-role="canvas-provenance-bar"
        >
          <div class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-[880px] flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3">
            <h1
              class="order-1 min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-lg font-semibold sm:flex-none sm:shrink-0"
              data-role="canvas-title"
            >
              {@canvas.title || "Untitled canvas"}
            </h1>

            <p
              class="order-5 min-w-0 basis-full truncate text-xs text-neutral sm:order-2 sm:grow sm:basis-0"
              data-role="canvas-crumb"
            >
              {provenance_crumb(%{
                org_name: @org_name,
                space_name: @space_name,
                author_name: @author_name,
                canvas: @canvas
              })}
            </p>

            <%!-- `--color-neutral`, deliberately not primary: read-only is a
               fact about this page, not a feature of it. --%>
            <span
              class="badge badge-outline order-2 ml-auto shrink-0 border-neutral/40 text-neutral sm:order-3 sm:ml-0"
              data-role="canvas-read-only-pill"
            >
              Read only
            </span>

            <%!-- `--color-primary` on hover and active only, never as text on
               white. --%>
            <button
              type="button"
              id={"canvas-copy-link-" <> @canvas.id}
              phx-hook="CopyToClipboard"
              data-clipboard-text={@canvas_url}
              data-role="canvas-copy-link"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs order-3 shrink-0 text-neutral hover:text-primary active:text-primary sm:order-4"
              aria-label="Copy link to this canvas"
              title="Copy link"
            >
              <span class="hero-link size-4"></span>
              <span class="hidden sm:inline">Copy link</span>
            </button>

            <%!-- THE WAY OUT, rightmost. Stage 3's bar had none: someone
               opening a pasted link in a fresh tab has no back history and no
               route anywhere. Same `hero-x-mark` as the in-chat overlay's
               close control (`live/chat/partials.ex`, `canvas_overlay/1`), so
               the same object carries the same control in both places.

               An `<a>`, not a `navigate` link: `/chat` lives in a different
               `live_session`, and the full HTTP round-trip is what re-runs
               `:enforce_external_scope`.

               `shrink-0` and rightmost, so at 375px it squeezes the title
               further. That is the correct trade: an exit outranks a longer
               title. The destination follows the viewer's grant — see
               `assign_close_path/3`. --%>
            <a
              href={@close_path}
              data-role="canvas-close"
              class="btn btn-ghost btn-xs order-4 shrink-0 text-neutral hover:text-base-content sm:order-5"
              aria-label="Close canvas"
              title="Close canvas"
            >
              <span class="hero-x-mark size-4"></span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </header>

        <%!-- The document sits DIRECTLY on `--color-base-100`: no card, no grey
           backdrop. Card-on-grey reads as an embedded preview of something
           living elsewhere, which is the wrong message for a page whose job is
           to make a shared canvas feel first-class.

           `max-w-[880px]` because canvases carry tables and mermaid diagrams;
           a narrower measure strangles them.

           Rendering delegates to the same `CanvasRenderer.canvas_document/1`
           the chat pane and task sheet call, so `Renderer.render_node/1` stays
           untouched and a canvas looks identical in both places. That is
           citation parity.

           `canvas-inert` is the scoped wrapper that makes `action_row`,
           `form` and `checklist_item` nodes render flat here: neutral text,
           no hover lift, `cursor-default`, buttons reduced to outline. They
           stay VISIBLE. Hiding them would break the same parity: an agent
           writes "two open actions", the human follows the link and counts
           none. Defined in `assets/css/app.css`; the class is unconditional
           because this whole surface is read-only for everyone. --%>
        <article
          class="canvas-inert mx-auto w-full max-w-[880px] px-4 py-8 sm:px-6"
          data-role="canvas-body"
        >
          <PlatformWeb.Chat.CanvasRenderer.canvas_document
            canvas={@canvas}
            dom_id_base="canvas-standalone"
          />

          <%!-- Mono earns its place here: it makes "a specific revision of a
             specific document" feel addressable, which is the whole point of
             a shareable URL. --%>
          <p
            class="mt-10 text-center font-mono text-xs text-neutral"
            data-role="canvas-revision-stamp"
          >
            rev {@revision} · {@canvas.id}
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
